Name: systemAppearance

Type: property

Syntax: get the systemAppearance

Summary:
Determins if the system appearance is dark or light mode. 

Introduced: 9.6

OS: mac, windows, android, html5

Platforms: desktop, mobile

Example:
if the systemAppearance is "dark" then
    set the backColor of me to "black"
    set the foreColor of me to "white"
else
    set the backColor of me to "white"
    set the foreColor of me to "black"
end if

Description:
The <systemAppearance> property returns either "dark" or "light" depending on
whether the application is running in a dark mode context or not. On
Windows it follows the "app mode" of Windows' colour settings. On Linux the
property returns "light", unless the <useDesktopAppearance> is true: then it
follows the desktop's light or dark setting (GNOME's "color-scheme", or a GTK
theme whose name says "dark").

In an HTML5 standalone it returns "light" unless the page was built with
**Appearance** set to **Follow the browser** in the Web pane of the
Standalone Application Settings: then it follows the browser's colour scheme
(the CSS prefers-color-scheme), and the <systemAppearanceChanged> message is
sent when that changes.

References: systemAppearanceChanged (message),
useDesktopAppearance (property)
